# Lady Madonna, bridge (figure 127.3)
# Sixteen bars, two to a chord, with the bassline Pollack types underneath walking in half notes:
# two bass notes to each bar (D C | B A over d, G F | E D over G, and so on).
# Bass octaves follow the stepwise descent (D3 down to D2); in the a-minor bars it turns and climbs
# A B C E. Pollack types no bass for the last phrase, so that system has the chords only.
# He puts the V of the third phrase under the F; it belongs to the G chord, as in the first phrase.
# Last phrase carries two keys of analysis (C: I; a: III ii V), and the "4 --> 3" is the
# suspension over the E chord, resolving at the start of its second bar.
#? Bass octaves: E2-D3 range chosen per the guidelines; on the record the line may sit an octave apart in places (e.g. the climb A B C E).
